  "body": "The maintainer asked for this research on 2026-11-18.\n\nContext: automatic nesting is in #2156 (research) and #2158 (NFP and bottom-left-fill placer). Router grain and vacuum rules are in #2398 and #2404. This issue asks if a different method can place the parts: rasterization on the GPU.\n\nQuestion: can a GPU shader do the placement calculation of a nest for router, plasma, waterjet and laser tables?\n\nOpen questions for the research:\n- Method: render each part and the sheet to a bitmap. Test a position with a shader (overlap count, minimum gap, kerf offset). Find the literature on raster nesting and on dilation or distance-field methods.\n- Accuracy: a bitmap has a resolution limit. Can the method keep a safe gap (kerf, tool radius, lead-in) with no overlap? What resolution does a 3 m sheet need for 0.1 mm? Can a final exact check with the vector geometry (`camcraft-geometry`) remove each risk?\n- Speed: compare with the NFP method of #2158. Estimate the gain for 50, 200 and 1000 parts and for the rotation set.\n- Platform: the project must build on Windows, Linux and macOS, and in the browser build (wgpu). Which GPU API and which compute or render path works on all of them? What happens when no GPU exists (CPU fallback)?\n- Determinism: the same input must give the same layout on each machine (`#2164`). Do GPU results differ between drivers? How do we pin this?\n- Layer rule: where does the code live (`camcraft-geometry` or a new crate)? Which dependency would it add? A new dependency needs a decision entry.\n- Features: grain (#2398), vacuum zones (#2404), holes that hold parts, remnant sheets (#2164).\n\nDone means: comments on this issue answer each question above, with sources. The research ends with a recommendation (use, do not use, or use only as a first filter before the exact check) and the follow-up issues, each with one `P*` and one `effort:*` label. Then close this issue.\n\nUse only published literature and general knowledge (Clean-Room Protocol).\n",
